<details><summary>Solution - grinding problems</summary>$$ \color{red} \spadesuit\color{red} \boxed{\textbf{Answer.}}\color{red} \spadesuit $$    
We claim that the boy can sit on all seats if and only if  $\boxed{\textbf{n is even}}$ .    $$ \color{blue} \clubsuit\color{blue} \boxed{\textbf{Construction.}}\color{blue} \clubsuit $$    
Let us demonstrate one such arrangement for  $n = 10$ , which clearly generalizes to all even  $n$ :  
[asy]size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(18pt));
real r = 0.17;
pen p = black; 
int[] numbers = {1, 8, 6, 4, 2, 10, 9, 7, 5, 3};

for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
    real angle = 90 - i * 36;
    pair pos = dir(angle);

    draw(circle(pos, r), p);
    label(" $" + (string)(numbers[i]) + "$ ", pos, p);
}[/asy]
Observe that the boy alternates between even and odd seats as he goes around.  
Each pair of consecutive numbers in this sequence adds up to  $11$ , and after completing two moves (one to an even seat and one back to an odd seat), the boy effectively “shifts” one position forward on the circle. Repeating this, he eventually visits every seat exactly once before returning to the starting one.  
[asy]
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(18pt));
real r = 0.17;
pen p = black;
int[] numbers = {1, 8, 6, 4, 2, 10, 9, 7, 5, 3};
pair[] positions = new pair[10];
for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
    real angle = 90 - i * 36;
    positions[i] = dir(angle);
    draw(circle(positions[i], r), p);
    label(" $" + (string)(numbers[i]) + "$ ", positions[i], p);
}
int[][] connections = {{0,1},{1,9},{9,2},{2,8},{8,3},{3,7},{7,4},{4,6},{6,5}};
for (int i = 0; i < connections.length; ++i) {
    pair start = positions[connections[i][0]];
    pair end = positions[connections[i][1]];
    pair dir = end - start;
    real dist = length(dir);
    pair unitDir = dir / dist;
    pair adjustedStart = start + unitDir * r;
    pair adjustedEnd = end - unitDir * r;

    if (i % 2 == 0) 
        draw(adjustedStart--adjustedEnd, red, Arrow);
    else
        draw(adjustedStart--adjustedEnd, blue, Arrow);
}
[/asy]  $$ \color{magenta} \bigstar\color{magenta} \boxed{\textbf{Why odd } n \textbf{ fail.}}\color{magenta} \bigstar $$    
Assume, for the sake of contradiction, that such a sequence is possible when  $n$  is odd.  
Let the  $\textit{length}$  of a move be the number of seats the boy skips clockwise to reach the next one. Since the move lengths are distinct and range from  $1$  to  $n-1$ , their total sum is    $$ 1 + 2 + \cdots + (n-1) = \frac{n(n-1)}{2}. $$    
This total length represents the net number of seats moved after the full journey, so    $$ \frac{n(n-1)}{2} \equiv 0 \pmod n $$    
must hold for the boy to return to his original seat. But for odd  $n$ , we have    $$ \frac{n(n-1)}{2} \equiv \frac{n-1}{2} \pmod n \neq 0, $$    
which means he ends up at a different seat than where he started - impossible if he is to visit each seat exactly once.  
Hence, no valid arrangement exists for odd  $n$ , and the only possible values are all even  $n$ .</details>

