*A different solution:* (the idea of an odd cycle in the complement graph is the same, but casework after that is much faster):
[rule]
Assume contrary. Let  $G$  be the given graph and  $\overline{G}$  be its complement. Note that  $\overline{G}$  must not be bipartite, as otherwise the larger of the two components would have  $\ge 6$  vertices. Let  $\mathcal C = \{c_1,c_2,\ldots,c_n\}$  be the smallest odd cycle of  $\overline{G}$ . Because of the minimality of  $\mathcal C$ , we have 


- Other than edges of  $\mathcal C$ , all other edges between vertices of  $\mathcal C$  must be in  $G$ .
- Any vertex not in  $\mathcal C$  can be connected to at most two of it vertices (wrt  $\overline{G}$ ), and those two vertices  $c_i,c_j$  must also satisfy  $i - j \equiv \pm 2$  (mod  $n$ ). 
[/list]
We now try to find  $9$  vertices which have a lot of edges in  $\overline{G}$ , which gives rise to the following Lemma:**<span style="color:#f00">Lemma:</span>**  $\overline{G}$  must not contain a subset of  $9$  vertices which can be divided into a odd cycle and some matchings. **<span style="color:#00f">Proof: </span>** Otherwise considering those  $9$  vertices gives a contradiction (as they cannot be a sub  $K_5$  in  $G$ ).  $\square$ Now the critical case  $n= 7$ , but we first resolve other cases:**Case 1:**  $n \le 5$ . 

As  $G$  contains no  $K_6$  so we would be able to find distinct vertices  $\{a_1,b_1\},\ldots,\{a_k,b_k\}$  with  $a_ib_i \in \overline{G}$  for all  $i$ , where  $k = \frac{7-n}{2}$  (by first finding  $\{a_1,b_1\}$ , then  $\{a_2,b_2\}$  and so on). Now let  $S = \mathcal C \cup \{a_1,b_1\} \cup \cdots \{a_k,b_k\}$  and  $T = G \setminus S$  (where  $|S| = 7$  and  $|T| = 5$ ). Because of the Lemma, we obtain  $T$  must form a  $K_5$  in  $G$ .  For any distinct  $u,v \in T$ , consider the  $9$  points  $S \cup \{u,v\}$ . Since this has a sub  $K_5$  in  $G$  so we obtain  $u,v$  have a common neighbour in  $\{a_1,b_1\}$  wrt  $G$ . In particular, any two vertices in  $T$  have a common neighbour in  $\{a_1,b_1\}$  (wrt  $G$ ). Moreover, note that any vertex in  $\{a_1,b_1\}$  must not be connected to all vertices of  $T$  (wrt  $G$ ), as otherwise we get a  $K_6$ . These two things cannot simultaneously hold, and thus we get a contradiction.**Case 2:**  $n \ge 9$ .

Because of the Lemma,  $|\mathcal C| \ne 5$  and thus  $|\mathcal C| = 11$ . Let  $\{u\} = G \setminus \mathcal C$ . Then using the two properties of  $\mathcal C$  we easily obtain a  $K_6$  in  $G$ .

Now we finally consider  $n = 7$ . Let  $T = G \setminus \mathcal C$  (this identical to the set  $T$  of Case 1). Again, because of our Lemma  $T$  must form a  $K_5$  (wrt  $G$ ). By second property of  $\mathcal C$ , for each  $u \in T$ , we can choose  $f(u)$  such that other than  $c_{f(u) \pm 1}$ , all other vertices of  $\mathcal C$  are connected  to  $u$  in  $G$  (possibly  $c_{f(u) \pm 1}$  might also be connected to  $u$ ).  **<span style="color:#f00">Claim: </span>** modulo  $7$ , we can choose a  $i$  and three distinct vertices  $u,v,w \in T$  such that  $f(u),f(v),f(w) \subseteq \{i,i+2,i+4\}$ . 
<span style="color:#00f">**Proof:**</span> Assume contrary and consider the multiset  $ M = \{f(u) : u \in T\}$ . For any  $j$ , we know at most two elements are in both the set  $\{j,j+2,j+4\},\{j+1,j+3,j+5\}$ . As  $|M| = 5$ , so  $j+6 \in M$ . As this holds for all  $j$ , so  $1,2,\ldots,7 \in M$ , which is a clear contradiction.  $\square$ Now the  $6$  vertices  $u,v,w,c_i,c_{i+2},c_{i+4}$  for a  $K_6$  in  $G$ , which completes the proof.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:#f0f">Remark: </span>** The bound of  $12$  is strict. Here are two constructions of  $\overline{G}$  if  $12$  is replaced by  $11$ :
