If  $Q(x)$  is constant then  $P(x)$  is constant, so assume  $\deg Q(x)\ge 1$ . Let us fix  $Q(x)$ . We prove the problem statement by strong induction on  $\deg(P(x))$ . We can shift  $P(x),Q(x)$  by a constant, hence we can assume that  $P(0)=Q(0)=0$ . Substitute  $y=0$ , implying that  $Q(x)\mid P(x)$ . Hence  $P(x)=T(x)Q(x)$  for a polynomial  $T(x)\in\mathbb R(x)$ . Hence  $$ Q(x)-Q(y)\mid T(x)Q(x)-T(y)Q(y) $$  so
\begin{align*}
Q(x)-Q(y)&\mid T(x)Q(x)-T(y)Q(y)+T(y)(Q(x)-Q(y))
&=(T(x)-T(y))Q(x).
\end{align*}
Now   $$ \gcd(Q(x)-Q(y),Q(x))=\gcd(Q(x),Q(y))=1 $$  as these are polynomials of different variables, so  $Q(x)-Q(y)\mid T(x)-T(y)$ . Since  $\deg T(x)<\deg P(x)$ , we are done by induction hypothesis.

I like this problem!  Here's a solution I don't think has appeared in this thread before.  I'm going to use  $A$ -indexing instead of  $C$ -indexing, and let  $I_b$  and  $I_c$  denote the  $B$  and  $C$  excenters, respectively.

Let  $M$  and  $N$  be the midpoints of minor and major arc  $\widehat{BC}$ , respectively.  Furthermore, let  $O_b$  and  $O_c$  denote the circumcenters of  $\triangle I_cAP$  and  $I_bAP$ , respectively.  By a well-known spiral similarity lemma,  $P$  is the center of spiral similarity  $\mathcal S$  sending  $\overline{I_bI_c}$  to  $\overline{O_bO_c}$ .
