First suppose  $n = rs$  is composite with  $1 < r, s < n$ . Then splitting the  $b_i$  into groups of  $r$  and setting each group equal to a different number is OK: the expression is always an  $r$ th power.

Now suppose  $n$  is a prime: we show the hypothesis cannot hold. Suppose that the distinct numbers in  $\{b_1, \dots, b_n\}$  are  $d_1, \dots, d_r$  and that they appear with multiplicities  $\lambda_1, \dots, \lambda_r$  respectively. Then the sum of the  $\lambda_i$  is the prime  $n$ , whence  $\gcd(\lambda_1, \dots, \lambda_r) = 1$  as  $r > 1$ .

Select sufficiently large (bigger than any difference  $b_i - b_j$ ) pairwise distinct primes  $p_1, \dots, p_r$ , and let  $k$  be a positive integer with
\begin{eqnarray*}
k & \equiv & -d_1 + p_1 \pmod{p_1^2}
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots
k & \equiv & -d_r + p_r \pmod{p_r^2}
\end{eqnarray*}
If  $P = (b_1 + k) \cdots (b_n + k)$ , then  $\nu_{p_1}(P) = \lambda_1, \dots, \nu_{p_r}(P) = \lambda_r$ . Since  $\gcd(\lambda_1, \dots, \lambda_r) = 1$ ,  $P$  cannot be a perfect power.

