I claim that the sum of the  $200$  numbers *cannot* be equal to  $24^{2022}.$  

Let the numbers be  $a_1, \ldots, a_{200}$  from left to right. Assume for the sake of contradiction that   $$ a_1+\ldots+a_{200}=24^{2022}. $$   Firstly, observe that for all positive integers  $x,$  we have
\begin{align*}
    17x \equiv 0 \pmod{17} &\implies 18x \equiv x \pmod{17} 
    &\implies 9x \equiv \frac{x}{2} \pmod{17}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, we have
\begin{align*}
    a_1+\ldots+a_{200}=24^{2022} &\implies a_1(1+\ldots+9^{199}) \equiv 24^{2022} \pmod{17}.
\end{align*}
Now, by Fermat's Little Theorem, note that
\begin{align*}
    1+\ldots+9^{199} &= \frac{9^{200}-1}{8} 
    &\equiv 3^{400}-1 
    &\equiv (3^{16})^{25}-1 
    &\equiv 1^{25}-1 
    &\equiv 1-1 
    &\equiv 0 \pmod{17},
\end{align*}
so it follows that   $$ 24^{2022} \equiv 0 \pmod{17}, $$   which is absurd. 

Hence, our original supposition that the sum of  $a_1, \ldots, a_{200}$  equals  $24^{2022}$  must have been false, as claimed.  $\square$

